// An Int is 64 bits, so a shift distance is 0 to 63. Shifting by 64 is not "everything falls off the
// end" — it is UNDEFINED in LLVM, and on x86 the hardware masks the distance to six bits, so `x << 64`
// silently becomes `x << 0` and the value comes back unchanged. A wrong answer that looks like a
// working program is the one outcome this language exists to prevent.
//
// A literal is refused here; a computed distance is checked at runtime
// (tests/panic/shift_distance_checked_at_runtime.bx).
print(shift_left(1, 64));
